# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 has "beautiful" and "above_duty → ground_elsewhere"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 have "beautiful" and "above_duty → ¬true_maxim"; they differ when only "it is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love and sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order", "we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, as if independent of the command, doing of our own good pleasure what we think needs no command", "our conduct follows the true moral maxim suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men" and "the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to" are true, which the first allows
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "it is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love and sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order", "we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, as if independent of the command, doing of our own good pleasure what we think needs no command", "our conduct follows the true moral maxim suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men" and "the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to" are true. Is S1, "It is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love to them and from sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order; but this is not yet the true moral maxim of our conduct which is suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men, when we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, like volunteers, and, as if we were independent on the command, to want to do of our own good pleasure what we think we need no command to do.", true then?
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 has "under_discipline" and "under_discipline → ground_in_law"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 has "under_discipline" and "under_discipline → O(ground_in_law ∧ ¬ground_elsewhere)"; they differ when only "we stand under a discipline of reason" and "in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it" are true, which the first allows; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 has "under_discipline" and "under_discipline → ground_in_law" and "ground_elsewhere → ¬ground_in_law"
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read kant-practical-4.logic.r2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 as what ought to be or is permitted: its "must" does not say which it means
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "we stand under a discipline of reason", "in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it" and "the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to" are true and "it ought to be that (in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it and it is not so that the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to)" is false. Is S2, "We stand under a discipline of reason and in all our maxims must not forget our subjection to it, nor withdraw anything therefrom, or by an egotistic presumption diminish aught of the authority of the law (although our own reason gives it) so as to set the determining principle of our will, even though the law be conformed to, anywhere else but in the law itself and in respect for this law.", true then?
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 has "ground_in_law → ¬ground_elsewhere" and "true_maxim → ground_in_law"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 has "under_discipline ∧ subjects → only_duty"; they differ when only "our conduct follows the true moral maxim suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men", "we stand under a discipline of reason", "in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it" and "we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign" are true, which the first allows; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 has "ground_in_law ∧ subjects → only_duty"
kant-practical-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 has "above_duty → ¬true_maxim"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 have "only_duty"; they differ when only "we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, as if independent of the command, doing of our own good pleasure what we think needs no command" is true, which the first allows
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 puts it at S1, "It is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love to them and from sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order; but this is not yet the true moral maxim of our conduct which is suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men, when we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, like volunteers, and, as if we were independent on the command, to want to do of our own good pleasure what we think we need no command to do."; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law."
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S1, "It is a very beautiful thing to do good to men from love to them and from sympathetic good will, or to be just from love of order; but this is not yet the true moral maxim of our conduct which is suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men, when we pretend with fanciful pride to set ourselves above the thought of duty, like volunteers, and, as if we were independent on the command, to want to do of our own good pleasure what we think we need no command to do.", rather than S3, "Duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law."?
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: kant-practical-4.logic.r2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it, then it is not so that the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 does not
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it, then it is not so that the determining principle of our will is set somewhere else than in the law and respect for it, even though the law be conformed to"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
