kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if our conduct follows the true moral maxim suitable to our position amongst rational beings as men, then in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U3: kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (we stand under a discipline of reason and we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign), then duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2 does not
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (we stand under a discipline of reason and we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign), then duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U4: kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it and we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign), then duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law"; kant-practical-4.logic.r2 does not
kant-practical-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U4: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-4.logic.r2.2 and kant-practical-4.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (in all our maxims the determining principle of our will is set nowhere but in the law itself and in respect for it and we are subjects in the moral kingdom, not the sovereign), then duty and obligation are the only names that we must give to our relation to the moral law"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
